Marketplace catalog freshness and restore safety
===============================================

A marketplace plugin executes with backend privileges. Pin the publisher's
Ed25519 public key and retain HTTPS verification. A signature proves the bytes
came from that key; acceptance also requires signed serial, generated_at and
expires_at fields. The consumer refuses expired or future-issued catalogs,
serial rollback, and reuse of one serial for different canonical content.
The first-party registry published signed serial 1 on 2026-09-24 with the
existing pinned publisher identity and new immutable package versions. Its
signed expiry is 2026-12-23T10:03:03Z; publishers must renew before expiry with
a strictly increasing serial. Verify the live signature and timestamps rather
than treating this dated record as a permanent freshness guarantee. No key
rotation or unsigned production exception is automatic.

Migration 019 adds a durable global catalog state and provenance on catalog
rows. Existing rows keep NULL provenance: sync a valid current catalog before
installing or updating them. Already running plugins are not stopped by a
withdrawal, expired catalog, failed sync, or this migration. A valid empty
signed catalog withdraws all offers, without uninstalling running copies.
Malformed or conflicting catalogs fail as one transaction; there is no partial
snapshot with a misleading advanced serial. Concurrent syncs serialize in SQL.

If the registry cannot be reached, the bundled seed supplies browse-only
metadata on a never-synced installation. It never overwrites existing entries,
revives withdrawals, or replaces an accepted remote snapshot. Expiry continues
to apply offline. Sync/install/update error messages state the reason for a
refusal; the UI also displays the sync result, including seed-only operation.

The accepted identity binds the configured registry URL and publisher key.
Changing either after the first accepted snapshot fails closed. A deliberate
publisher migration needs a reviewed transfer of trust and serial history;
do not clear the state row or lower a floor just to make a refusal disappear.
MARKETPLACE_ALLOW_UNSIGNED is an explicit development/private-registry opt-out
of signatures only. Freshness and serial ordering still apply in that mode.

Database recovery requires an independent floor
----------------------------------------------

A restored database also restores its old serial state. Database persistence
alone cannot prove that a later withdrawal occurred. Before backup and before
any restore, retain the highest accepted serial in an independently managed
operator record, associated with the publisher/feed identity. Read-only query:

  SELECT identity, serial, payload_sha256, generated_at, expires_at
  FROM core.marketplace_catalog_state WHERE scope = 'global';

Set MARKETPLACE_MIN_SERIAL in the deployment environment to at least that
independently retained serial BEFORE starting restored API/worker services.
The shared Compose environment passes it to each backend service. Keep this
record and configuration outside the database backup being restored, protect
it from rollback, and use the same value on every node. Defaults to 0 for new
installations; invalid/negative/out-of-range values disable sync/install/update.

After recovery, verify the identity/floor and UTC clock, then sync a signed,
unexpired catalog whose serial meets the floor. If the retained state or
publisher is uncertain, leave catalog installs/updates disabled and reconcile
with the publisher. Do not infer trust from an old backup's plugin URLs.
This mechanism is not an external transparency log, trusted-time service, key
revocation service or automatic recovery of a lost independent floor.

Downgrading migration 019 refuses to discard a nonzero accepted serial. Preserve
and reconcile that security history before a reviewed software rollback. Test
customer restores and multi-node configuration separately; the synthetic tests
do not establish customer RTO/RPO or a full enterprise deployment guarantee.
